<h1 class="article-title">Seminar to Discuss Karosta Canal Pollution and Its Effects on the Environment and Human Health</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>A seminar and discussion entitled “The Effects of Pollution on Human Health and the Environment: Historical and Everyday Pollution” will take place at Liepāja University on 13 October 2022 at 10:00.</p><p>Experts from the Latvian Institute of Aquatic Ecology and Liepāja City Council will describe the effects of pollution on the environment and human health and the work carried out to reduce it. Experts from Liepāja University and the Liepāja Special Economic Zone will focus on the practical aspects of reducing pollution in Karosta Canal and the effects of those measures on the environment and human health.</p><p>This is the third educational seminar in the information campaign for the Liepāja SEZ Authority project “Remediation of Historically Contaminated Sites in Karosta Canal, Port of Liepāja”. The seminar aims to educate the public about the impacts of historical and everyday pollution on the environment and human health, highlighting present and future risks and the steps being taken to mitigate them. Karosta Canal is part of everyday life and socioeconomic development in Liepāja. Cleaning the canal will improve environmental quality and biodiversity in the canal and the ecology and water quality of the Baltic Sea, while giving residents of Liepāja and Karosta and visitors a cleaner, healthier and more pleasant place to live, grow and relax.</p><p>Historically, military base activities have significantly affected environmental quality in Karosta Canal. Pollution includes petroleum products such as benzene, xylene and hydrocarbons, as well as heavy metals including mercury, arsenic, lead, zinc, copper and cadmium, concentrated in the canal’s silt and sand deposits.</p><p>The Karosta Canal ecosystem is degraded, with very limited flora and fauna. Pollution in the canal also affects nearby perch, which migrate to other waters such as Lake Liepāja. Perch found there have markedly elevated mercury concentrations. Pollution therefore spreads beyond the local area through food chains, affecting other levels of the ecosystem and ultimately people.</p><p>The project continues work to clean Karosta Canal that began in 2015. Earlier stages removed man-made debris such as scrap metal, concrete and timber and established a specially equipped containment area for contaminated sediments at the eastern end of the canal, where some pollutants have already been deposited. The work significantly improved the canal’s ecological status: concentrations of petroleum products and heavy metals in sediments fell severalfold, but remain above the limits set by Cabinet regulations. To meet environmental improvement objectives for Karosta Canal and the Baltic Sea, remediation work will remove 202,247 m³ of non-compliant sediment from the canal bed, containing 400 tonnes of contaminated deposits. The project is financed through the Norwegian Financial Mechanism’s 2014–2021 programme “Climate Change Mitigation, Adaptation and Environment”. Total costs amount to EUR 5,117,647.05, including EUR 4,350,000 in Norwegian Financial Mechanism funding.</p><p> </p><p>Working together for a green Europe!</p></div>
